This folder holds what ARTS keeps of Evans' PolRadTran radiative transfer
models RT3 and RT4: the licence and description of the package, and his
benchmark scripts and data, which ARTS's tests use.

Origin: PolRadTran.tar from https://nit.coloradolinux.com/polrad.html
(sha256 7b0eff794319330321e27a6f312588a289ed080bb266500dcecf485f6a7cff9d),
under the MIT licence in LICENSE, which must be kept with the code: ARTS's
RT3 and RT4 (src/core/polradtran) are C++ ports of the tar's Fortran (see
doc/arts/dev.rt3.rst and doc/arts/dev.rt4.rst).  README is Evans'
description of the package.  RT3 (randomly oriented particles, solar and
thermal sources, all Fourier azimuth modes, I, Q, U and V) is Evans and
Stephens (1991), JQSRT 46, 413-423; RT4 (horizontally oriented particles,
thermal source, I and Q) is Evans and Stephens (1995), J. Atmos. Sci. 52,
2058-2072.

Kept from the tar, unchanged: LICENSE, README, Evans' four benchmark scripts
runmietest, runtesta (RT3), runtestr and runtestc (RT4), which define their
problems and hold his expected outputs, and cl340d14.dda, the RT4
scattering file that runtestc reads.  The VDISORT comparison with RT3, the
RT4 benchmark test in src/tests/polradtran and the Python tests in
tests/core/rt3 and tests/core/disort read them.  The C++ RT3 tests carry
copies of the tables and series they need.

ARTS keeps none of the Fortran.  Its outputs for cases that cover every
branch are kept as the references of the port's tests, in
src/core/polradtran/rt3/test and src/core/polradtran/rt4/test.  The history
of this file lists the ARTS changes to the Fortran.
